\f1\fs22 Sarah never got sick. Never a sore throat, or runny nose, or headache. She doubted she\'92d ever had bad breath. But today, some three weeks since Tony was rounded up by TRAMPS or whatever Public Safety Broadcasting called themselves, she had every symptom at once. And being blankly unaccustomed to such fragility, she felt her throat excoriated and acid-washed; her nose a clagged, high-pressure hose; her skull a multi-bladed food processor set loose in her brain; her spittle tasted like a sewer stocked with minced bison droppings and cockatoo feathers.\
The d\'e9cor of her mansion-arrest did nothing to help. Blood-red walls, frilly curtains, and ceiling-mounted broadcast screens (one of which had a crazed bullet hole in it) all conspired against her sanity.\
She called for Nancy, the housekeeper, who helped her out of bed and into a bathroom whose gold fittings and petrified-crossword tiles worked together to drive another set of blender blades between her eyes.\
Sarah staggered past the toilet, taking refuge in the circular ceramic cloister of the shower, where she hurled indiscriminately.\
\'93I\'92ll call the doctor,\'94 soothed Nancy.\
\'93No,\'94 Sarah choked out, jaws clamped against another rush. \'93This will pass.\'94\
It did, each day, until the same hour. Sarah refused the house doctor for four days, trusting she\'92d recover by sheer force of previous robust health. On day five Nancy rang for a house-call anyway.\
Sarah doubled over in bed, waiting for the doctor. Projecting herself forward to a time when she\'92d again be hearty and hale brought no comfort. Sarah was ill-prepared for discomfort.\
She jerkily grabbed at the bedside remote and stabbed a button. The overhead screens flickered to life. She immediately regretted the distraction; another repeat of 
\f2\i Curb Your Individualism
\f1\i0  was playing, with Tony as guest of honour. Since his disappearance after the Vegas Show, and Piotr Dunhilton\'92s declaration of him as Cloudborne Enemy #1, the \'93Chanta Confesses\'94 episode was in endless rotation on CurbTV.\
Sarah watched every day, unable to look away, fighting tears as Tony bore up under goon after goon, never begging, never pleading, never bargaining.\
Goon: 
\f2\i You kidnapped Sarah Jacobson.
\f1\i0 \
Tony: 
\f2\i I don\'92t know who that is.
\f1\i0 \
Goon: 
\f2\i Tell us where you\'92re keeping Sarah Jacobson.
\f1\i0 \
Tony: 
\f2\i I don\'92t know where she is.
\f1\i0 \
The worst, and the sweetest.\
After the first week, Sarah realised there was more than one episode. Three were obvious, then three more joined the rotation. A new one aired every two days, but Tony always looked fresh and whole at the start\'97an editing trick, Sarah reasoned, splicing a single interrogation session into segments. Yet as new episodes kept coming, she noticed continuity errors: Tony\'92s prison overalls grew steadily more bloody, more soiled, even as his face and fingers seemed flushed with renewal.\
Chilled, appalled and ultimately angry, Sarah drew a gun from the side-table drawer, and blew a hole in the second screen.\
Nancy rushed to Sarah\'92s side, but when she realised another broadcast screen had been sacrificed, she remarked, \'93Oh, it\'92s so good to hear life in the house again.\'94\
