\f1\fs22 She rolled onto her back, rubbed her eyes, and pushed up to a slouch, pillows helping. She blinked, waited.\
\'93I\'92m Dr John,\'94 he said. \'93This is one of my entourage, Dr Nick.\'94\
\'93Hello,\'94 Sarah mumbled, rearranging her bed-hair. \
His gaze lingered a beat too long, as though he found her fetching even after the vomiting. He wore what looked like a mash-up between a doctor\'92s coat and a 
\f2\i karate
\f1\i0  
\f2\i keikogi
\f1\i0 . His hair was perfect in late-\'9260s casual: feathered, layered, matte, slightly insubordinate at the fringe, with lamb-chops that suggested moderate, stylish rebellion.\
Dr John sat diagonally one-cheeked on the bed, and gently lifted Sarah\'92s chin and peered into her eyes. Dr Nick, a passably well-dressed Cloudborne, hovered back in John\'92s shadow.\
\'93I understand you\'92ve been having nausea, headaches, vomiting?\'94\
\'93Yes,\'94 she whispered.\
\'93Fever?\'94\
\'93I don\'92t think so.\'94\
\'93Are you autistic?\'94 Dr John\'92s tone shifted to stern.\
\'93No?\'94\
\'93Good. We of the Change of Habit series are terrible with autism.\'94\
Sarah\'92s nose crinkled.\
\'93No alpha-1-antitrypsin deficiency?\'94\
\'93Certainly not.\'94\
\'93Good,\'94 Dr John said. He made a vague flourish; Dr Nick, wordless, placed a baggie of white powder in his hand. Dr John dangled it.\
\'93What\'92s that?\'94\
\'93Dr Nick\'92s Mix,\'94 said Dr John. \'93Good for what ails you.\'94\
Sarah never believed the stories of Elvi drug addiction, preferring the more medically and culturally based reasonings of Grayling Corporation\'92s User Manual:\
